---
/*
  Tell me your dev disaster.

  Rendered on demand rather than prerendered, for the same reason the report page is: it
  has to know whether the reader is signed in before it draws a form that needs a sign in,
  and a page built once and served from a CDN cannot know that. Low traffic, so an on
  demand render costs nothing worth measuring.

  The form posts to /api/disasters/. It is a real form with real fields rather than a
  script driven one, so the no JavaScript path is the only path and there is nothing to
  degrade.

  The mockup's rail said "TITLE: I'll write it" and the notice said "I write a title".
  Decision 25 says the title and slug are generated on submit and editable afterwards, so
  the copy here says that instead. A public promise the system will not keep is worse
  than a slightly less charming sentence.

  The anonymity control is a checkbox, not a button with aria-checked. A button needs
  script to change state, and this page is not allowed to need script.
*/
export const prerender = false;

import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const params = Astro.url.searchParams;
const signedIn = Astro.locals.profile !== null;

/*
  Outcomes of the form post, since the form works with JavaScript off and the answer comes
  back as a redirect rather than as a message an island drew.
*/
const SENT: Record<string, { tone: 'ok' | 'bad'; text: string }> = {
  '1': {
    tone: 'ok',
    text: "Got it. It's in the queue with a title drafted for it. I read everything that comes in. If story notifications are on, you'll hear when it publishes."
  },
  signin: { tone: 'bad', text: 'That needs a sign in. Sign in and send it again, the box will still be there.' },
  consent: { tone: 'bad', text: 'Both boxes at the bottom need ticking before I can run it.' },
  short: { tone: 'bad', text: "That's shorter than a story. Tell me what broke and what fixed it." },
  long: { tone: 'bad', text: "That's longer than anything I can publish. Send me the part you still think about." },
  slow: { tone: 'bad', text: "That's three today, which is my reading limit rather than yours. Tomorrow." },
  off: { tone: 'bad', text: "Submissions aren't saving right now. Try later, or email it to me." },
  bad: { tone: 'bad', text: "That didn't come through. Try again." },
  failed: { tone: 'bad', text: "That didn't save. Try again, or email it to me." }
};

const sent = SENT[params.get('sent') ?? ''] ?? null;
---

<Base
  title="Tell me your Dev disaster"
  active="dd"
  description="Somewhere in your git history is a commit you still think about. Write it down."
>
  <main id="main">
    <div class="shell read">
      <aside class="rail">
        <dl>
          <div><dt>Review time</dt><dd>Usually a day</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Title</dt><dd>Drafted for you</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Length</dt><dd>Whatever it takes</dd></div>
        </dl>
        <span class="fine">Nothing publishes until I've read it.</span>
      </aside>

      <article>
        <header class="storyhead">
          <span class="path">dev-disasters<span class="sep">/</span><b>new</b></span>
          <h1>What's the one you <span class="squiggle"><span>still think about?</span></span></h1>
          <p class="lede">
            No form fields to fight with. No severity to pick. Just tell it the way you'd
            tell it to somebody at a bar who also writes code.
          </p>
        </header>

        <form class="form submit-form" method="post" action="/api/disasters/">
          {
            sent && (
              <p class={sent.tone === 'ok' ? 'notice submit-sent' : 'notice submit-sent bad'} role="status">
                {sent.text}
              </p>
            )
          }

          {
            !signedIn && (
              <p class="notice submit-signin">
                <a href="/auth/signin/?next=%2Fsubmit%2F">Sign in with GitHub</a> before you
                send it. Not so I know who you are on the page, because you can still publish
                anonymously, but so the story is attached to an account and the badge has
                somewhere to land. That account is also where you control story notifications.
              </p>
            )
          }

          <div class="field">
            <label for="story">The story</label>
            <p class="help">
              Start wherever it starts. What broke, what you tried, what actually fixed it,
              and what you do differently now. Markdown works, and code fences are welcome
              if the query is the punchline.
            </p>
            <textarea
              id="story"
              name="story"
              rows="14"
              required
              placeholder="It was a Tuesday and I was the guy who knew the database..."></textarea>
            <p class="help count">No minimum, no maximum</p>
          </div>

          <div class="switch-row">
            <input class="switch" type="checkbox" id="anon" name="anonymous" checked />
            <div>
              <label class="switch-label" for="anon">Publish anonymously</label>
              <span class="switch-help">
                Your name and handle stay off the page. The badge still lands on your
                profile, and story notifications can still reach you, because both follow
                the account rather than the byline.
              </span>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="field">
            <label>Before you send it <span class="opt">plain English, no lawyers</span></label>
            <label class="consent">
              <input type="checkbox" name="consent_scrubbed" required />
              <span>
                This happened to me, or I was there for it. I've taken out anything that
                would identify my employer, my coworkers, or a customer. If I missed
                something, take it out for me.
              </span>
            </label>
            <label class="consent">
              <input type="checkbox" name="consent_publish" required />
              <span>
                You can publish it here, edit it for length and clarity but never for
                meaning, and talk about it in a video.
              </span>
            </label>
          </div>

          <p class="notice">
            Here's what happens next. It lands in a queue only I can see, with a title and
            a web address drafted for it automatically. I read it, fix the title if the
            machine got it wrong, check it for anything that identifies somebody, and file
            it under a severity. If story notifications are on, the site emails you when it
            publishes and if it reaches the front page. A story I do not publish does not
            send an email, so your account still shows every submission and where it stands.
          </p>

          <div class="form-foot">
            <button class="btn primary" type="submit" aria-disabled={!signedIn}>Send it in</button>
            <span class="fine">
              {signedIn ? 'You can edit it until I publish it.' : 'Sign in first and this wakes up.'}
            </span>
          </div>

          <p class="fine submit-fine">
            The long version lives in the <a href="/terms/">submission terms</a>. It says
            the same thing with more commas. The <a href="/conduct/">code of conduct</a>
            applies here too.
          </p>
        </form>
      </article>
    </div>
  </main>
</Base>

<style>
  .submit-form {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
  }
  .submit-fine {
    margin-top: -0.5rem;
  }
  .submit-sent {
    border-color: var(--sev-info);
  }
  .submit-sent.bad {
    border-color: var(--sev-error);
  }
</style>
